course: biology
lesson: 181
title: Strange islands: Komodo dragons, the dodo, red crabs, kakapos, giants and dwarfs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An island is like a small world cut off by the sea: only the animals that can fly, swim or float there arrive, and then they change on their own for thousands of years.
explain: With little food, big animals slowly become smaller over many generations, so elephants on some islands shrank to the size of a pony.
explain: With no hunters around, small animals can become bigger and birds can stop flying, like the heavy kakapo parrot that walks through the New Zealand forest at night.
explain: That is why island animals are so strange, and also why they are in such danger when ships bring rats, cats and pigs they have never had to fear.
fact: The Komodo dragon of Indonesia is the largest living lizard, up to about three metres long; it ambushes deer and pigs, and its bite has venom that stops blood from clotting.
fact: People once thought germs in the Komodo dragon's mouth killed its prey, but scientists found it has venom glands in its jaw.
fact: The dodo was a big flightless pigeon from Mauritius; it died out in the late 1600s after sailors brought rats, pigs and monkeys that ate its eggs, and people hunted it too.
fact: The dodo was not stupid; it had lived with no hunters, so it had no reason to fear people.
fact: Every year tens of millions of red crabs on Christmas Island march from the forest to the sea to lay eggs, when the rains come and the moon is right; people close roads and build crab bridges for them.
fact: The kakapo is a heavy, flightless parrot of New Zealand that is active at night; New Zealand had no land mammals hunting on the ground, so it could give up flying; there are only about two hundred and fifty, and every one has a name.
fact: On islands such as Sicily and Crete, elephants once shrank to the size of a pony; some scientists think their skulls, with one big hole for the trunk, gave rise to stories of one-eyed giants.
fact: On islands with no predators, some small animals become giants, like the giant rats of Flores and giant tortoises; this is called island gigantism.
fact: The Jaragua dwarf gecko of the Caribbean is one of the smallest reptiles, small enough to curl up on a coin.
fact: Hawaiian honeycreepers all came from one kind of finch and now have many beak shapes, like curved beaks for sipping nectar; many are endangered because mosquitoes spread bird malaria, and many now survive only high in the cold mountains, where mosquitoes do not live well.
fact: The dragon's blood tree of Socotra Island is shaped like an umbrella, which shades its roots and catches fog; its red sap was once used as paint and medicine.
fact: A kiwi lays an egg about one fifth of the mother's own weight, so the chick hatches strong and can look after itself early, and the kiwi is the only bird with nostrils at the tip of its beak, used to smell worms.
fact: Island foxes on California's Channel Islands are about the size of a house cat; they were saved from near extinction partly by moving golden eagles away.
exercise: What is the largest living lizard? | The Komodo dragon | The green iguana of America | The chameleon of Madagascar | The Gila monster of America
exercise: What does a Komodo dragon's bite have in it that stops blood clotting? | Venom from glands in its jaw | Only the germs from rotten meat in its mouth | Sticky glue | Nothing at all
exercise: What kind of bird was the dodo? | A big flightless pigeon | A kind of penguin that lived on beaches | A small ostrich from Africa | A giant duck that could not swim
exercise: Why did the dodo die out? | Rats, pigs and monkeys brought by sailors ate its eggs, and people hunted it | It was too stupid to find food and starved | The island sank into the sea after a huge earthquake and a giant wave | A cold winter froze them all
exercise: Why did the dodo not run away from sailors? | It had lived with no hunters and had never learned to fear them | It was too fat to move | It wanted to be their pet and follow them home on the ships | It could not see people
exercise: Why do millions of red crabs on Christmas Island cross the roads? | They march from the forest to the sea to lay eggs | They are running away from forest fires on the hills | They are looking for cars to eat | They move house every week
exercise: What tells red crabs when to start their march? | The rains and the moon | A whistle from the park rangers | The first snowfall of winter | The longest day of summer
exercise: Which of these is true about the kakapo? | It is a heavy parrot that cannot fly and is active at night | It is the fastest flying parrot in the whole world, faster than a falcon | It lives in the Sahara Desert | It is a kind of owl that hunts mice
exercise: Why could the kakapo give up flying? | New Zealand had no land mammals hunting on the ground | It was too cold to fly | Its wings were eaten away by tiny insects living in its feathers | People clipped its wings
exercise: What happened to elephants that lived on small islands like Sicily long ago? | They became as small as a pony | They grew twice as tall as elephants on land | They learned to swim away to other islands | They grew thick woolly fur, like mammoths
exercise: What is island gigantism? | Small animals growing very big on islands with no predators | Islands growing bigger every single year | Giants living on islands | Big animals swimming across the sea to islands and staying there for good
exercise: How small is the Jaragua dwarf gecko? | Small enough to curl up on a coin | As big as a cat | As long as an arm | The size of a big round dinner plate
exercise: Where did all the Hawaiian honeycreepers come from? | One kind of finch that reached the islands | Parrots blown over from Australia | Hummingbirds from America | Penguins that swam up from cold Antarctica
exercise: Why is the dragon's blood tree shaped like an umbrella? | It shades its roots and catches fog | It keeps the rain off the birds that nest in it | Its branches were cut by people | It is pushed flat by elephants
exercise: How big is a kiwi's egg compared with its mother? | About one fifth of her weight | Smaller than a pea, like an insect egg | Bigger than the mother herself | Exactly the same size as a hen's egg
exercise: Where are a kiwi's nostrils? | At the tip of its beak | On top of its round head | Just behind its two eyes | Under its wings, near the tips
exercise: How big is an island fox from California? | About the size of a house cat | As big as a wolf | As big as a horse | As small as a mouse, the tiniest of all
test: In Ice Age a gang of silly dodos fight over a melon and fall off a cliff one after another. What is the truth? | The dodo was not stupid; it just had no reason to fear people, and sailors' animals ate its eggs | Dodos really did die out by falling off cliffs | Dodos were tiny birds that lived in the snow | Dodos are still alive in the Arctic
test: «my cousin says komodo dragons kill with dirty spit, all germs innit» What is right? | No, they have venom glands in their jaws that stop blood clotting | Yes, it is only the germs from old meat in their mouths | No, they kill by breathing fire | Yes, and they have no teeth
test: «3 komodo dragons in the zoo, how many legs and tails altogether» | 15 | 12 | 9 | 18
test: What would happen if rats reached an island where a bird nests on the ground and cannot fly? | The rats would eat its eggs and chicks, and the bird could die out | The bird would quickly learn to fly | The rats would make friends with the bird and help it find food | Nothing, because rats cannot eat eggs
test: An old Greek shepherd finds a huge skull with one big hole in the middle of its face. What was it most likely? | The skull of a dwarf elephant, the hole being for its trunk | The skull of a real one-eyed giant from the old Greek stories | The skull of a whale | The skull of a dragon
test: «2 dwarf elephants and 1 normal elephant, how many trunks and ears» | 9 | 6 | 3 | 12
test: Ships brought cats to an island where a parrot walks about at night. Why is this so dangerous for the parrot? | The parrot never had ground hunters, so it does not run or fly away | Cats eat only parrot food, so they would steal all the seeds and fruit | Cats make the forest too noisy | The parrot is allergic to cats
test: «saw crabs everywhere on the road, someone tell em to go back to the sea» What is really happening? | Red crabs are walking from the forest to the sea to lay their eggs | Crabs are lost and cannot find their way back to the water after the rain | Crabs are running from a storm | Crabs are looking for food on the road
test: «kakapos are just lazy parrots, they could fly if they tried» What is right? | No, the kakapo is too heavy to fly; it lost flight because it had no ground hunters | Yes, they fly at night when nobody sees | Yes, they fly every winter to Australia | No, kakapos are not birds at all
test: Why do many island birds in Hawaii now live only high up in the mountains? | Mosquitoes that carry bird malaria do not live well in the cold high places | The birds like the view | There is more nectar on the beach | The birds are afraid of the sea
test: Why does a kiwi chick need a big egg? | A big egg makes a strong chick that can look after itself early | Kiwis lay eggs only to scare enemies | The egg must float in the sea | Kiwi chicks are born as big as their parents and leave home on the first day
exam: Identify the term which denotes the process whereby small animals inhabiting predator-free islands attain an unusually large body size. | Island gigantism | Island dwarfism | Hibernation | Seasonal migration
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the circumstances of the extinction of the dodo? | Animals introduced by sailors consumed its eggs, and it was hunted by people | It perished owing to its want of intelligence | It was extinguished by a volcanic eruption alone | It migrated to another continent
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Komodo dragon. | It is the largest living lizard and its bite contains venom | It is a species of crocodile that inhabits rivers | It kills its prey solely by means of oral bacteria | It is a flightless bird of New Zealand
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which the kakapo of New Zealand lost the capacity for flight. | Its homeland lacked mammalian predators hunting on the ground | It was domesticated by early settlers | Its feathers were destroyed by the cold of the southern winters | It acquired the habit of swimming instead
